<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oscar Wilde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Oscar Fingal O'Fflahertie Wills Wilde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oscar Wilde was an Irish author, poet and playwright and a key figure of the Aestheticism movement of the late 19th century. Born to Anglo-Irish intellectuals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> and Magdalen College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. He wrote his only novel, The Picture of Dorian Gray (1890), and four society comedies, among them The Importance of Being Earnest (1895). His libel suit against the Marquess of Queensberry, father of his lover Lord Alfred Douglas, led to his own conviction for gross indecency and two years' hard labour, served from 1895 to 1897. On his release he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, wrote The Ballad of Reading Gaol (1898) and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde.jpg" alt="Oscar Wilde" width="330" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Napoleon Sarony / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oscar_Wilde_by_Napoleon_Sarony._Three-quarter-length_photograph,_seated_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Oscar Wilde was born on 16 October 1854 at 21 Westland Row, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, the second of three children of Jane Wilde, née Elgee, and Sir William Wilde. He left Portora with a royal scholarship to read classics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> from 1871 to 1874, where his tutor J. P. Mahaffy inspired his interest in Greek literature. At Magdalen College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, he read Greats from 1874 to 1878, and he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1878.</p><p>In 1882 the impresario Richard D'Oyly Carte invited him to lecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Wilde arrived on 2 January 1882, and a tour planned for four months lasted almost a year because of its commercial success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Wilde wrote for The Pall Mall Gazette and other journals in 1885 to 1887, though he tired of journalism. He published The Happy Prince and Other Tales in 1888. The first version of The Picture of Dorian Gray was the lead story of Lippincott's Monthly Magazine in July 1890; its hero makes a Faustian bargain in which only his portrait grows old.</p><p>He wrote Salomé in French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His society comedies began with Lady Windermere's Fan, first performed on 20 February 1892 at St James's Theatre, which earned him £7,000 in its first year. By 1893 he was infatuated with Lord Alfred Douglas. His last play, The Importance of Being Earnest, turns on &quot;bunburying&quot;, the keeping of alternative personas in town and country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 February 1895 the Marquess of Queensberry left a card at Wilde's club accusing him of posing as a sodomite. Wilde brought a private prosecution for libel. Queensberry's lawyers hired detectives, and Wilde was arrested on 6 April 1895 for gross indecency. The first trial ended without a verdict; at the second he was convicted and sentenced to two years' hard labour. After his release he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, never to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> or Ireland. By 25 November 1900 he had meningitis, and he was conditionally baptised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> before he died on 30 November 1900.</p><p>He was buried at Bagneux and in 1909 moved to Père Lachaise Cemetery, where his tomb was designed by Sir Jacob Epstein. In 2017 he was among an estimated 50,000 men pardoned for acts no longer offences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oscar Wilde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Walter Scott used two stanzas in The Lay of the Last Minstrel (1805), Goethe three in the cathedral scene of Faust (1808), and <b>Oscar Wilde</b> wrote a sonnet on hearing it sung in the Sistine Chapel (1881).</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li></ul></section>
